course: history
lesson: 4
title: Mesopotamia: wheels, laws and empires
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Once there were cities, people needed ways to move goods, settle quarrels and rule large lands, and Mesopotamia tried all three.
explain: The wheel appeared about 5,500 years ago, first for potters and soon for carts, though people had already farmed and built towns for thousands of years before it.
explain: Think of your school rules pinned on the wall so nobody can say they did not know. King Hammurabi of Babylon had about 280 laws carved on a tall stone for everyone to see, though they were harsher on the poor and on slaves than on the rich.
explain: Kings like Sargon built empires, many peoples ruled by one king. Babylon grew into a famous city with blue-tiled gates, the Assyrians ruled from great palaces, and finally the Persians built the largest empire the world had yet seen.
fact: Wheels appeared about 5,500 years ago; potters used spinning wheels, and soon wheels were put on carts.
fact: The wheel was not the first invention: people had farmed, built towns and made pottery for thousands of years before it.
fact: About 4,300 years ago Sargon of Akkad conquered the cities of Mesopotamia and made one of the first empires, many peoples ruled by one king.
fact: King Hammurabi of Babylon, about 3,750 years ago, had about 280 laws carved on a tall stone so everyone could see them.
fact: Some of Hammurabi's laws said the punishment should match the harm, an eye for an eye, but the laws were harsher on poor people and slaves than on the rich.
fact: Babylon was one of the largest and most famous cities of the ancient world, with mighty walls and the blue-tiled Ishtar Gate.
fact: Old writers said Babylon had Hanging Gardens on terraces (wide steps of earth, one above another), one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world, but no one has found clear remains, so some historians doubt they were there.
fact: Mesopotamia had little wood, stone or metal, so merchants traded grain and cloth for timber, copper and precious stones from far away.
fact: Mesopotamian priests watched the stars and the moon and kept records of them for centuries, which helped them make calendars and predict what the sky would do.
fact: The Assyrians built a strong, feared empire with great palaces at Nineveh decorated with stone pictures of lion hunts.
fact: The Assyrian king Ashurbanipal collected a huge library of clay tablets; baked clay does not rot or burn away easily, so many survive, which is how we can still read the Epic of Gilgamesh.
fact: About 2,500 years ago the Persian king Cyrus the Great took Babylon and built the largest empire the world had yet seen.
fact: The Persian Empire was well organised and fairly tolerant for its time, not the monster army shown in the film 300.
exercise: About how long ago did the wheel appear? | About 5,500 years ago | About 550 years ago | About 55,000 years ago | About 300,000 years ago
exercise: Who used spinning wheels before carts had wheels? | Potters | Sailors on ships | Soldiers | Bakers in towns
exercise: Was the wheel the very first invention? | No, farming, towns and pots came long before | Yes, it was invented even before people had fire | Yes, it came before stone tools | No, the car came first
exercise: What did Sargon of Akkad create? | One of the first empires | The first writing on clay | The first wheel for carts | The first Olympic Games
exercise: What is an empire? | Many peoples ruled by one ruler | A single small village on a river | A group of equal friends | A city with no ruler at all
exercise: Why were Hammurabi's laws carved on a tall stone? | So everyone could see them | So nobody could ever read them | To use as a door for the palace | To mark where the river was
exercise: About how many laws did Hammurabi's stone have? | About 280 | About 10 laws | About 2,800 | Exactly 1 law
exercise: What did "an eye for an eye" mean in Hammurabi's laws? | The punishment should match the harm | Everyone should have their eyes tested | Kings could see everything | Thieves should be forgiven
exercise: Were Hammurabi's laws equal for everyone? | No, they were harsher on the poor and slaves | Yes, rich and poor were treated exactly the same | Yes, slaves had more rights | No, they were harsher on the king
exercise: What was the Ishtar Gate famous for? | Its blue tiles | Its gold roof | Being made of ice blocks | Being the tallest tower
exercise: What were the Hanging Gardens of Babylon? | Gardens said to grow high up on terraces | Gardens hung from ropes under the city's bridges | A forest on the moon | Flowers growing inside a pyramid
exercise: Why did Mesopotamian merchants travel far? | Their land had little wood, stone or metal | They had too much timber at home to burn | Their kings banned farming | They wanted to see the ocean before they died
exercise: What did Mesopotamian priests record for centuries? | The movements of the stars and moon | The scores of football games | The weight of every person | The names of all the fish
exercise: What decorated the Assyrian palaces at Nineveh? | Stone pictures of lion hunts | Paintings of steam trains | Gold statues of sacred cats | Glass windows of Christian saints
exercise: Why is Ashurbanipal's library important today? | Its clay tablets let us read Gilgamesh | It had the first printed books | It was the first library with computers | It held all the books of Rome
exercise: Who took Babylon and built a huge empire about 2,500 years ago? | Cyrus the Great of Persia | Julius Caesar of Rome | Hammurabi of Babylon | Tutankhamun, the boy king of Egypt
test: In the film 300 the Persians look like monsters. What is closer to the truth? | Their empire was well organised and fairly tolerant | They really were giant monsters, just as the film shows | They had no empire at all | They were Vikings in disguise
test: mate says the wheel was the first thing ever invented, before farms even. true? | No, farming and towns came thousands of years earlier | Yes, wheels came before fire | Yes, cavemen drove carts round on wooden wheels before they farmed | No, the plane came first
test: If a law is written on a stone in the square, what can a judge no longer do? | Make up a different rule on the day | Read the law out loud | Punish anyone at all | Go to the square
test: A rich man and a poor man do the same wrong under Hammurabi. What usually happens? | The poor man is punished more harshly | They get exactly the same punishment, fair for all | The rich man is punished more | Nobody is punished
test: Why might some historians doubt the Hanging Gardens were real? | No clear remains have been found in Babylon | They were found on the moon | Every writer of the time said they were fake | The gardens are still growing there today
test: A clay library survives 2,600 years but a paper one burns. Why does clay last? | Baked clay does not rot or burn away easily | Clay is magic | Paper was not allowed to be kept in libraries by law | Clay floats on water
test: Babylon has grain but no timber for roofs. What does it do? | Trades grain and cloth for timber from afar | Builds roofs out of grain | Stops building houses at all | Grows great forests in the desert overnight with magic
test: One king ruling many cities and peoples is called what? | An empire | A small village | A democracy | A tribe of hunters
test: Why would star records kept for centuries be useful? | They help make calendars and predict the sky | They show where to dig for gold | They tell you the weather tomorrow exactly | They help find lost sheep
test: My teacher said, no wait, she said Babylon was in Egypt. Where was it? | In Mesopotamia, in today's Iraq | In Egypt by the Nile | In Greece by the sea | In China by the Yellow River
test: Which is in the right order from oldest to newest? | Sargon, then Hammurabi, then Cyrus | Cyrus, then Sargon, then Hammurabi | Hammurabi, then Cyrus, then Sargon | Cyrus, then Hammurabi, then Sargon
exam: Identify the ruler under whose authority approximately 280 laws were inscribed upon a stone stele for public inspection. | Hammurabi of Babylon | Sargon, king of Akkad | Cyrus the Great of Persia | Ashurbanipal of Assyria
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the application of the said laws? | Penalties were more severe for the poor and the enslaved than for the wealthy | All persons were treated with complete equality | The wealthy were subject to the more severe penalties | No penalties whatsoever were prescribed
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the merchants of Mesopotamia engaged in long-distance trade. | The region was deficient in timber, stone and metal | The region possessed a surplus of timber | Agriculture was prohibited therein | The merchants were obliged to visit the ocean
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the empire established by Cyrus the Great. | It was the most extensive empire hitherto seen and comparatively tolerant | It consisted of a single city | It was governed by the Senate of Rome | It was destroyed prior to its foundation
